.Dd $Mdocdate: August 2 2026 $
.Dt Fugu::Signal 3p
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm Fugu::Signal
.Nd signal handlers for graceful shutdown
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Bd -literal
use Fugu::Signal;

my $sig = Fugu::Signal->new;
$sig->add_cleanup(sub ($signal) { unlink $tempfile });
$sig->setup_graceful_exit('INT', 'TERM');

# or, to decide when to stop:
$sig->setup_interrupt_flag('INT', 'TERM');
until ($sig->interrupted) {
    do_some_work();
}
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
installs signal handlers so that a signal can interrupt a program
safely.
The program does not leave temporary files, child processes or
half-written state behind.
.Pp
There are two shapes.
.Fn setup_graceful_exit
runs the cleanup handlers and exits from inside the signal handler.
This shape is correct for a program that can stop its work at all
times.
.Fn setup_interrupt_flag
only sets a flag.
The program must do a check of the flag and stop where a stop is safe.
.Pp
In each shape, the object keeps the previous handlers.
.Fn restore
puts them back,
and the destructor calls
.Fn restore .
Thus a scoped object leaves the process in its initial state.
.Pp
Each manager owns its handlers, its cleanups and its interrupt flag.
Two managers in one process do not see each other's state.
The installed handlers close over the object, so a handler always
finds the manager that installed it.
.Ss new
.Fn new "%args"
creates a signal handler manager.
It does not install handlers.
.Pp
This is the argument:
.Bl -tag -width "exit_statusXX"
.It Fa exit_status
The exit code of a graceful exit.
The default is 130, the conventional code for
.Dv SIGINT .
.El
.Ss setup_graceful_exit
.Fn setup_graceful_exit "@signals"
installs a handler for each named signal.
The handler sets the interrupt flag of this manager,
runs the cleanup handlers of this manager,
and exits with
.Fa exit_status .
.Ss setup_interrupt_flag
.Fn setup_interrupt_flag "@signals"
installs a handler for each named signal.
The handler sets the interrupt flag and returns.
No other action occurs until the program reads the flag.
.Ss add_cleanup
.Fn add_cleanup "$handler"
adds a code reference that runs,
with the signal name as its argument,
before
.Fn setup_graceful_exit
exits.
The handlers run in the sequence in which you add them.
Each handler runs inside an
.Ic eval .
Thus a handler that dies does not stop the others.
.Ss restore
.Fn restore
puts back the handlers that this object replaced.
.Ss interrupted
.Fn interrupted
reports if this manager saw a handled signal.
.Ss reset_interrupted
.Fn reset_interrupted
clears the interrupt flag of this manager.
.Ss check_interrupted
.Fn check_interrupted
reports if any live manager saw a handled signal.
It is a plain function, not a method.
It serves code that runs far from the object, for example a poll loop
deep in a library.
Code that holds the object uses
.Fn interrupted .
.Ss reset_all_interrupted
.Fn reset_all_interrupted
clears the interrupt flag of every live manager.
It is a plain function, not a method.
Tests use it between cases.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn new
returns a signal handler manager.
.Fn setup_graceful_exit ,
.Fn setup_interrupt_flag ,
.Fn add_cleanup ,
.Fn reset_interrupted
and
.Fn restore
return the object.
Thus the calls can chain.
.Pp
.Fn interrupted
and
.Fn check_interrupted
return true if a handled signal came.
.Sh EXAMPLES
This example stops between items and not in the middle of one:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
my $sig = Fugu::Signal->new;
$sig->setup_interrupt_flag('INT', 'TERM');

for my $item (@items) {
    last if $sig->interrupted;
    process($item);
}
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
No method dies.
If a cleanup handler dies, the module catches the error and ignores it.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr perlipc 1 ,
.Xr kill 2 ,
.Xr sigaction 2 ,
.Xr Fugu::Process 3p
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Dick Olsson Aq Mt hi@senzilla.io
.Sh CAVEATS
The cleanup list survives the run.
A second signal that comes after the start of a graceful exit finds the
same handlers and runs them again.
Thus a cleanup handler must be safe to run twice.
.Pp
.Fn check_interrupted
answers for the whole process.
It returns true when any live manager saw a signal, and it cannot tell
the caller which one.
.Pp
The handlers are standard Perl signal handlers and run between opcodes,
not immediately.
A program that a system call blocks sees the signal when the call
returns.
